{"name":"Sabal Palm","hex":"#496a4e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sabal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any palm of the genus Sabal of American dwarf fan palms; usually called palmetto."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sabal"},{"term":"palm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various evergreen trees from the family Palmae or Arecaceae, which are mainly found in the tropics.","A branch or leaf of the palm, anciently borne or worn as a symbol of victory or rejoicing."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The inner and somewhat concave part of the human hand or other primate hand that extends from the wrist to the bases of the fingers.","The corresponding part of the forefoot of a nonprimate mammal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/palm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sabal palmetto","blocks":[{"text":"Sabal palmetto, also known as cabbage palm, cabbage palmetto, sabal palm, palmetto palm, blue palmetto, Carolina palmetto, common palmetto, and swamp cabbage, is one of 15 species of palmetto palm.\nIt is native to the Southeast United States Coastal Plain, the Yucatán Peninsula in Mexico, and the West Indies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Spalmetto2.JPG/330px-Spalmetto2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabal_palmetto"},{"title":"Sabal","blocks":[{"text":"Sabal is a genus of New World palms. Currently, there are 17 recognized species of Sabal, including one hybrid species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/SabalPalm.jpg/330px-SabalPalm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabal"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Native_range_of_Sabal_palmetto.png/500px-Native_range_of_Sabal_palmetto.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_camp